# The browser this project drives lives here, and it is ~300 MB.
# It is a build artefact, not source: `node scripts/get-browser.js` puts it back.
# `git clean -xfd` will remove it, which is the trade for keeping the project
# self-contained rather than sharing one install across projects.
.browser/

# What Settings → Automation → Install puts here: the engine packages.
# Regenerated by that button, never edited, never committed.
node_modules/

# Where forge.js writes engines an agent has built. Ignored for the reason
# node_modules/ is: this is code a program wrote at runtime, so committing it
# would mean reviewing generated source instead of choosing to check it in. The
# things worth keeping are forge.js and the test, and both of those are source.
#
# Deleting this directory costs nothing — every engine in it can be asked for
# again — and it is the one place the agent's output lands, so it is also the
# first place to look when asking what an agent actually wrote.
engines/

# Holds the API key. Never commit it — rotate any key that has been committed.
data/agent-config.json
data/automation.json

# Settings → Agent runtimes. Ignored for the same reason as the two above, and it
# is the one that would hurt most: every record here is an endpoint plus an
# optional bearer key, written by whoever typed them into the form.
#
# The redaction in ai/runtimes/store.js means the key never reaches the browser,
# which is a different and weaker promise than never reaching a shared history.
# Once a secret is in a commit it is in every clone, and the only remedy is to
# rotate it — so the file is excluded rather than trusted to be scrubbed.
data/runtimes.json

# The agent's own rules, and the changes waiting for a person to accept them.
#
# Ignored for the same reason as the two above: data/ is per-machine state, not
# source. rules.json is not only this machine's, though — it carries a history
# with a reason and an author on every entry, which is a record of what a person
# decided about their own agent and why. A `git add .` after accepting a change
# would put that in a shared history, and the whole premise of the feature is that
# applying a rule is a person's decision rather than something that happens.
#
# Sharing an edited rule is a deliberate act and wants an export, not an accident.
data/rules.json
data/rule-proposals.json

# scratch: chat uploads, swept an hour after they arrive
data/inbox/

# The credential the server issues for itself, mode 0600, rewritten on every
# start. It is a bearer secret for a loopback service: anything that can read it
# can drive the browser and the shell, so it is per-machine state like the two
# above. Committing one would publish a token that was live when it was written.
data/access-token

# `npm start > server.log`, or a background start that redirects. The server's
# log() goes to stdout, so whatever captures it captures a file that is different
# on every machine and says nothing about the code. Left out on purpose.
server.log
nohup.out
